          "question": "e-4708",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "Reducing $M$ to echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & \u223c[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -2 & -6 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 9 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & \u223c[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 9 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & \u223c[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041677/assets/36f5603649e8e185a03bf9c4e88180ec95ebb3b065e78b4d2d462b5ac2801bf4.png)\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final two equations $0x+0y+0z = 0,$ we can remove these equations from the system. So, our system now consists of just a single equation:\n\n$$\nx + y + 3z = 1\n$$\n\nThe basic variable is $x$, and we have two free variables $y$ and $z$. That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y,z.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\nx = 1-y-3z.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041677/assets/90f271b13648e97d322d31aa29c4a9cb647dc9d5c1b7d8d0eee23875d36480ab.png)\n\nwhere $y,z$ (the free variables) are arbitrary numbers.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1-y-3z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+y[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+z[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGeometrically, this is the equation of a plane in $\\mathbb R^3$, and any point on the plane is a solution to the system.",
